Let $\epsilon_B$ be the block idempotent of $B$ and let $1-\epsilon_B=\sum_{B'
e B}\epsilon_{B'}$. The block decomposition of $S$ makes $X\mapsto X(1-\epsilon_B)$ an exact functor that kills every module in $B$, so applying it to the class identity gives $0=\sum_\lambda a_\lambda[(1-\epsilon_B)W^{\lambda}]=\sum_{\lambda:\,W^{\lambda}
otin B}a_\lambda[W^{\lambda}]$ in $G_0$. The Weyl classes are linearly independent: this is the unitriangularity of the Kostka matrix recorded in [[lem-semistandard-weight-dimensions-detect-virtual-weyl-classes]], applied with the zero module. Hence every surviving coefficient $a_\lambda$ vanishes, that is, $a_\lambda=0$ whenever $W^{\lambda}
otin B$. This is Mathas, Lemma 5.35 with Lemma 5.30, printed pp. 57 and 60. No choice principle is used.
